# name: test/sql/regression/issue_302.test
# description: a connection the pool cannot create fails fast and says why (issue #302, spec 073)
# group: [mssql]
# issue: 302

# Environment variables required:
#   MSSQL_TESTDB_DSN - Connection string to test database
#
# The reporter's Azure SQL catalog could not open a NEW connection two hours
# after ATTACH -- the Azure AD token the pool factory had captured was 60
# minutes old -- and every symptom of that was
#
#   IO Error: MSSQL: Failed to acquire connection from pool (timeout)
#
# after the full acquire timeout. The token half needs Azure and an hour, and
# lives in the azure lane. This is the pool half, which needs neither: a
# factory that cannot succeed -- here, a wrong password -- used to be treated
# as an exhausted pool. Acquire waited for a Release that could not come, and
# the server's own reason, recorded in the connection's last_error_, was thrown
# away. Now: nothing is active, so the failure comes back at once, and it names
# what the server said.

require mssql

require-env MSSQL_TESTDB_DSN

# Long on purpose. Before the fix this is how long the statement below took
# to fail; after it, the timeout is never reached because nothing is active
# and waiting would only run out the clock.
statement ok
SET mssql_acquire_timeout = 30;

# Connection-string keys: last one wins, so this overrides the DSN's password.
# lazy_validation defers the login to the first statement, which is what makes
# the POOL do it rather than ATTACH.
statement ok
ATTACH '${MSSQL_TESTDB_DSN};Password=this-is-not-the-password' AS bad (TYPE mssql, lazy_validation true);

# The message carries the pool's name, says a connection could not be CREATED
# (not "timeout"), and quotes the server.
statement error
SELECT * FROM bad.dbo.SomeTable;
----
could not create a connection

statement error
SELECT * FROM bad.dbo.SomeTable;
----
Login failed for user

# mssql_exec goes through the same provider.
statement error
SELECT mssql_exec('bad', 'SELECT 1');
----
could not create a connection

# Nothing leaked into the pool by trying.
query II
SELECT total_connections, active_connections FROM mssql_pool_stats('bad');
----
0	0

statement ok
DETACH bad;
